    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d815">

                <pb facs="0937" n="925" sameAs="frus:frus1942v01/pg925"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00115A Pacific War/12: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Huddle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bern</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1942-02-14"
                            >February 14, 1942</date>.<lb/>[Received February 14—3:51
                        p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>587. American interests—Thailand. Department’s 217, January 26, 9 p.m. Swiss
                    Consul Bangkok telegraphs February 9 following (in English): <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Minister Foreign Affairs<anchor n="35" xml:id="d815-f1"/> answered
                            February 7 [as follows:] ‘State that the treatment of American officials
                            and nationals in Thailand has been warranted by circumstances and in
                            consonance with practice in other countries. No remedy is therefore
                            called for especially as a state of war now exists between Thailand and
                            United States of America’.”</p></quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Huddle</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d815-f1" n="35" xml:id="d815-f1-content">
                <p>Sri Thammathibet.</p>
